1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,793
An auditor goes through last quarter's
pull requests and stops at one.

2
00:00:08,833 --> 00:00:13,806
It is marked "merged". It has no approving
review. And according to the timeline,

3
00:00:13,846 --> 00:00:18,819
nobody pressed its merge button. The auditor
asks your CTO: your rules require an

4
00:00:18,859 --> 00:00:22,409
approval for every change to
main. How is this one merged?

5
00:00:22,749 --> 00:00:27,685
Nothing was bypassed and nobody cheated.
Another pull request, which contained these

6
00:00:27,725 --> 00:00:32,602
commits, was merged. GitHub then marked
this one as merged too, because its commits

7
00:00:32,642 --> 00:00:37,459
had become reachable from main. GitHub
documents this, including the sentence that

8
00:00:37,499 --> 00:00:42,198
matters to the auditor: it happens "even
if branch protection rules on that pull

9
00:00:42,238 --> 00:00:45,278
request were not satisfied".
Hold on to the auditor.

10
00:00:45,318 --> 00:00:47,944
You'll rebuild that pull
request in the demo.

11
00:00:49,784 --> 00:00:53,591
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:53,631 --> 00:00:58,762
A pull request, remember, is a proposal to
merge a head branch into a base branch.

13
00:00:58,802 --> 00:01:04,059
Today, two short sections of Chapter 17 that
belong together, because both are about

14
00:01:04,099 --> 00:01:07,022
pull requests that contain
other pull requests.

15
00:01:07,362 --> 00:01:13,205
Section 17.13, indirect merges: how a pull
request becomes "merged" by reachability.

16
00:01:13,245 --> 00:01:18,737
Section 17.14, stacked pull requests: a
deliberate chain of pull requests, each

17
00:01:18,777 --> 00:01:24,549
based on the branch below, and what happens
to the chain when a layer changes or is

18
00:01:24,589 --> 00:01:25,039
merged.

19
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:29,567
One replay, labs/ch17/stacked.sh,
with eight snippets.

20
00:01:29,607 --> 00:01:34,656
The GitHub side of stacking is
described from section 17.14 only.

21
00:01:34,696 --> 00:01:41,075
The stacked pull requests feature is in
public preview since the thirtieth of July

22
00:01:41,115 --> 00:01:47,260
2026, with the CLI extension github/gh-stack.
That extension is not part of the

23
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:53,679
GitHub CLI 2.88.1 used in this course, none
of its commands were run for the book,

24
00:01:53,719 --> 00:01:57,437
and I do not walk through
the preview interface.

25
00:01:57,777 --> 00:02:01,397
Labels: git rebase and git
rebase --onto are 🟡 CAUTION.

26
00:02:01,437 --> 00:02:06,854
git push --force-with-lease is 🔴 DANGEROUS.
Its five answers were given two videos

27
00:02:06,894 --> 00:02:12,311
ago, and it is appropriate here for your
own pull request branches after a rebase.

28
00:02:14,151 --> 00:02:15,564
After this video you can:

29
00:02:15,904 --> 00:02:20,698
Explain how a pull request can show as
merged although nobody pressed merge on it.

30
00:02:21,038 --> 00:02:25,266
Build a stack of two pull requests
and say what each one's base decides.

31
00:02:25,606 --> 00:02:28,022
Restack after a review
fix in the lower layer.

32
00:02:28,362 --> 00:02:32,826
Predict the state of the upper pull request
when the lower one is squash-merged and

33
00:02:32,866 --> 00:02:34,671
its branch deleted, and repair it.

34
00:02:35,011 --> 00:02:39,845
State the status of GitHub's stacked pull
requests feature as the section gives it.

35
00:02:41,685 --> 00:02:45,546
Indirect merges, with two branches
on screen, one on top of the other.

36
00:02:45,586 --> 00:02:50,005
In one sentence: GitHub marks a pull
request as merged whenever its head commits

37
00:02:50,045 --> 00:02:53,962
become reachable from its base branch,
by whatever route that happened.

38
00:02:54,002 --> 00:02:58,421
Reachable means you can get there from
the branch tip by following parent links.

39
00:02:58,761 --> 00:03:03,258
In the documentation's words: "A pull
request can be marked as merged if its head

40
00:03:03,298 --> 00:03:07,683
branch commits become reachable from the
base branch outside that pull request.

41
00:03:07,723 --> 00:03:12,165
This can happen when the same commits are
merged through another pull request or

42
00:03:12,205 --> 00:03:16,758
pushed directly to the default branch."
And then: "Pull requests merged indirectly

43
00:03:16,798 --> 00:03:21,351
are marked as merged even if branch protection
rules on that pull request were not

44
00:03:21,391 --> 00:03:25,217
satisfied." In the picture, M is the
merge of the upper pull request.

45
00:03:25,557 --> 00:03:29,801
Why does it matter? Reviews and checks
are properties of a pull request.

46
00:03:29,841 --> 00:03:34,799
Reachability is a property of commits. A
rule that requires an approval is satisfied

47
00:03:34,839 --> 00:03:39,798
by the pull request that was actually merged,
not by each pull request whose commits

48
00:03:39,838 --> 00:03:44,737
it contained. So when an audit asks who
approved a commit, you answer from the pull

49
00:03:44,777 --> 00:03:46,998
request that carried it onto the base.

50
00:03:47,338 --> 00:03:51,967
The chapter lists indirect merges next
to two other routes, --admin and bypass

51
00:03:52,007 --> 00:03:56,757
lists, as three ways a change reaches a
protected branch without the reviews the

52
00:03:56,797 --> 00:04:01,486
rules describe. Know all three before you
tell an auditor that every change was

53
00:04:01,526 --> 00:04:02,025
reviewed.

54
00:04:02,365 --> 00:04:06,129
Stacked pull requests: the same
two branches, built on purpose.

55
00:04:06,169 --> 00:04:11,201
In one sentence: a stack is a chain of pull
requests in one repository in which each

56
00:04:11,241 --> 00:04:16,031
targets the branch of the one below, so
that a large change is reviewed as small

57
00:04:16,071 --> 00:04:16,454
layers.

58
00:04:16,794 --> 00:04:21,376
Why would you do that? A reviewer can read
three pull requests of two hundred lines.

59
00:04:21,416 --> 00:04:25,889
Nobody reads one of six hundred well. And
the upper layers do not have to wait for

60
00:04:25,929 --> 00:04:28,530
the lower ones to merge
before they are written.

61
00:04:28,870 --> 00:04:34,144
How it works is nothing new. It is the
wrong-base case from the last video, used on

62
00:04:34,184 --> 00:04:39,330
purpose. The base decides what you see.
The upper pull request against main lists

63
00:04:39,370 --> 00:04:43,299
both layers. Against the branch
below, it lists its own layer.

64
00:04:43,639 --> 00:04:47,147
What GitHub's preview feature
adds, from its reference page.

65
00:04:47,187 --> 00:04:52,055
All branches must be in the same repository:
"Cross-fork stacks are not supported."

66
00:04:52,095 --> 00:04:56,609
"Every pull request in a stack is evaluated
against rules for the base of the

67
00:04:56,649 --> 00:05:01,340
stack", typically main: required reviews,
required status checks, CODEOWNERS and

68
00:05:01,380 --> 00:05:02,167
code scanning.

69
00:05:02,507 --> 00:05:07,239
A pull request can merge only when it and
"all pull requests below it" meet the

70
00:05:07,279 --> 00:05:12,252
requirements, and when "the stack has a
fully linear history between its branches".

71
00:05:12,292 --> 00:05:17,326
And the asynchronous merge API, generally
available since the first of October 2026,

72
00:05:17,366 --> 00:05:20,950
"is the only merge API that
supports stacked pull requests".

73
00:05:21,290 --> 00:05:26,244
Linear, in Git terms, means the lower
branch is an ancestor of the upper one.

74
00:05:26,284 --> 00:05:30,459
You can test that with one command,
git merge-base --is-ancestor.

75
00:05:30,799 --> 00:05:34,684
Two events break a stack, and they
are the two failure modes to know.

76
00:05:35,024 --> 00:05:39,953
First, a review fix lands on the lower
branch. The upper branch still sits on the

77
00:05:39,993 --> 00:05:43,144
old tip. The lower branch
is no longer its ancestor.

78
00:05:43,184 --> 00:05:48,114
The repair is then a rebase of the upper
layer onto the new tip of the lower one,

79
00:05:48,154 --> 00:05:52,654
and a forced push of the rewritten
branch. With more layers this cascades.

80
00:05:52,694 --> 00:05:57,746
The preview's button for this exists, and
the documentation notes that the button's

81
00:05:57,786 --> 00:06:02,839
commits "are not signed". In plain Git, git
rebase --update-refs moves every branch

82
00:06:02,879 --> 00:06:04,311
of the chain in one run.

83
00:06:04,651 --> 00:06:09,367
Second, the bottom layer is squash-merged,
replaced on main by one new commit.

84
00:06:09,407 --> 00:06:14,184
Then the upper layer is in the squash-then-reuse
situation from the last video,

85
00:06:14,224 --> 00:06:18,270
because it was built on commits
that never reached main as commits.

86
00:06:18,310 --> 00:06:20,099
What does GitHub do by itself?

87
00:06:20,439 --> 00:06:25,292
With the stack feature: "the remaining
branches are automatically rebased so the

88
00:06:25,332 --> 00:06:28,411
next pull request targets
the default base branch".

89
00:06:28,451 --> 00:06:33,121
Without it, GitHub only retargets: when
a merged head branch is deleted, pull

90
00:06:33,161 --> 00:06:36,913
requests based on it switch to
the merged pull request's base.

91
00:06:36,953 --> 00:06:39,054
The transplant is then yours to do.

92
00:06:39,394 --> 00:06:44,922
Quick quiz. No preview feature. The bottom
branch is squash-merged and deleted.

93
00:06:44,962 --> 00:06:48,869
What does GitHub do to the
upper pull request by itself?

94
00:06:48,909 --> 00:06:52,604
A: rebases it. B: only changes
its base. Your answer?

95
00:06:57,444 --> 00:07:02,125
B. It retargets, and nothing more. The
commits don't move until you move them, like

96
00:07:02,165 --> 00:07:02,410
this.

97
00:07:02,750 --> 00:07:07,170
When not to stack: layers that are not
dependent should be independent pull

98
00:07:07,210 --> 00:07:11,928
requests. A stack multiplies force pushes
and re-approvals, and a problem in the

99
00:07:11,968 --> 00:07:16,745
bottom layer delays every layer above. And
from the chapter's edge cases: stacked

100
00:07:16,785 --> 00:07:21,146
pull requests are a preview. Do not
make a release process depend on them.

101
00:07:22,986 --> 00:07:27,775
Picture floors of a building under construction.
Each floor is inspected separately,

102
00:07:27,815 --> 00:07:29,671
and each stands on the one below.

103
00:07:30,011 --> 00:07:34,883
Two things can happen to a lower floor. It
can be altered after the upper floor was

104
00:07:34,923 --> 00:07:39,796
built: then the upper floor has to be lifted
and set down again on the altered one.

105
00:07:39,836 --> 00:07:44,116
That is restacking. Or the lower floor
can be torn down and replaced by a

106
00:07:44,156 --> 00:07:47,785
prefabricated block of the same
shape: that is a squash merge.

107
00:07:48,125 --> 00:07:51,615
The block has the same rooms,
and none of the original beams.

108
00:07:51,655 --> 00:07:56,303
The upper floor is still bolted to beams
that are no longer part of the building,

109
00:07:56,343 --> 00:07:58,212
and must be moved onto the block.

110
00:07:58,552 --> 00:08:02,414
The model breaks for indirect merges,
and the break is instructive.

111
00:08:02,454 --> 00:08:07,306
In a building, signing off the top floor
does not make anyone think the floors below

112
00:08:07,346 --> 00:08:11,964
were inspected. On GitHub, merging the
top layer makes every layer below show as

113
00:08:12,004 --> 00:08:15,400
"merged", with whatever inspections
each one had or lacked.

114
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:21,225
First, the stack. The lower branch
has four commits from main.

115
00:08:21,265 --> 00:08:26,547
The upper branch adds two. Pull request 2
has the lower branch as its base, so its

116
00:08:26,587 --> 00:08:31,545
merge base is the tip of the lower branch,
c38d3bd, and it lists two commits.

117
00:08:31,885 --> 00:08:37,004
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper: squash
the lower layer onto main, delete its

118
00:08:37,044 --> 00:08:40,590
branch, and count what pull
request 2 lists against main.

119
00:08:40,630 --> 00:08:41,220
I'll wait.

120
00:08:46,060 --> 00:08:51,623
Next, the lower pull request has been
squash-merged: one new commit on main,

121
00:08:51,663 --> 00:08:57,817
77fc160, with no link to the lower branch.
The branch is deleted, and pull request 2

122
00:08:57,857 --> 00:09:02,462
targets main. Lay the ruler: the
merge base is back at 9a383e5.

123
00:09:02,502 --> 00:09:03,347
Six commits.

124
00:09:03,687 --> 00:09:08,599
And the repair: the two commits of the upper
layer, transplanted onto main with new

125
00:09:08,639 --> 00:09:08,838
IDs.

126
00:09:18,678 --> 00:09:23,234
Two layers, both pushed: the branch
feature/priority-routing, and on top of it

127
00:09:23,274 --> 00:09:24,354
feature/sla-timers.

128
00:09:26,694 --> 00:09:31,512
The upper layer has two commits of its
own. How many does a pull request from it

129
00:09:31,552 --> 00:09:34,427
against main list? And
against the branch below?

130
00:09:34,767 --> 00:09:39,855
Five against main: both layers. Two
against the branch below: its own layer.

131
00:09:39,895 --> 00:09:42,554
The base decides what the reviewer sees.

132
00:09:42,894 --> 00:09:47,210
Exit status 0: the lower branch
is an ancestor of the upper one.

133
00:09:47,250 --> 00:09:48,572
The stack is linear.

134
00:09:48,912 --> 00:09:51,424
Now a review fix lands
on the lower branch.

135
00:09:53,764 --> 00:09:57,489
One new commit on the lower
branch. Is the stack still linear?

136
00:09:57,829 --> 00:10:03,339
Exit status 1. The graph shows a fork: the
upper branch still sits on the old tip.

137
00:10:03,679 --> 00:10:07,094
The upper layer is replayed on
the new tip of the lower one.

138
00:10:07,134 --> 00:10:09,570
Linear again, and two
commits with new IDs.

139
00:10:09,910 --> 00:10:14,580
Notice the two pushes: the lower branch
only gained a commit, so its push is a

140
00:10:14,620 --> 00:10:18,686
fast-forward. The upper branch was
rewritten, so its push is forced.

141
00:10:18,726 --> 00:10:23,577
Under "dismiss stale approvals", both pull
requests lose their approvals, because

142
00:10:23,617 --> 00:10:25,087
the diff of each changed.

143
00:10:25,427 --> 00:10:29,470
Now the lower pull request is
squash-merged and its branch deleted.

144
00:10:29,510 --> 00:10:31,360
The upper one now targets main.

145
00:10:33,700 --> 00:10:38,403
The upper layer still has two commits of
its own. What does its pull request list

146
00:10:38,443 --> 00:10:38,637
now?

147
00:10:38,977 --> 00:10:44,355
Six. The four commits of the lower layer
are listed again, because the squash commit

148
00:10:44,395 --> 00:10:48,805
on main has no link to them. This
is the second state of the diagram.

149
00:10:48,845 --> 00:10:54,159
Now the repair: predict the three arguments
of the --onto command before I type it.

150
00:10:54,199 --> 00:10:55,320
Say them out loud.

151
00:11:00,160 --> 00:11:05,011
Only the upper layer is new. The command
reads: everything after the old lower

152
00:11:05,051 --> 00:11:10,216
branch, onto main. Note that the local
branch feature/priority-routing still exists

153
00:11:10,256 --> 00:11:13,539
in this clone and marks where
the upper layer begins.

154
00:11:13,579 --> 00:11:17,176
git fetch --prune removed only
the remote-tracking branch.

155
00:11:17,516 --> 00:11:21,431
Two commits, and a clean test merge.
A forced push republishes it.

156
00:11:21,771 --> 00:11:25,797
Last, the indirect merge, in a copy
where no branch was merged yet.

157
00:11:25,837 --> 00:11:29,742
Asha merges the upper pull request
into main with a merge commit.

158
00:11:32,082 --> 00:11:36,342
Only pull request 2 was merged. What
is the state of pull request 1?

159
00:11:36,682 --> 00:11:42,128
Exit status 0, and the range from main to
the lower branch is empty: every commit of

160
00:11:42,168 --> 00:11:44,348
the lower pull request is on main.

161
00:11:44,688 --> 00:11:47,613
On GitHub, pull request 1
would now show as merged.

162
00:11:47,653 --> 00:11:52,147
Nobody pressed its merge button, and
whatever review it still lacked was never

163
00:11:52,187 --> 00:11:55,112
given. That is the auditor's
pull request, rebuilt.

164
00:11:56,952 --> 00:11:58,600
Five mistakes to watch for.

165
00:11:58,940 --> 00:12:02,511
Reading "merged" on a pull request
as "reviewed and approved".

166
00:12:02,551 --> 00:12:07,286
Root cause: a pull request is marked merged
when its head commits become reachable

167
00:12:07,326 --> 00:12:11,362
from the base, by any route, even if
its own rules were not satisfied.

168
00:12:11,702 --> 00:12:16,554
Opening the upper layer of a stack against
main. Root cause: the base decides the

169
00:12:16,594 --> 00:12:20,420
range; against main the pull
request lists every layer below it.

170
00:12:20,760 --> 00:12:25,101
Pushing a review fix to a lower layer
and leaving the upper layers alone.

171
00:12:25,141 --> 00:12:30,142
Root cause: the lower branch is no longer
an ancestor of the upper one, so the stack

172
00:12:30,182 --> 00:12:30,982
is not linear.

173
00:12:31,322 --> 00:12:36,070
Squash-merging the bottom layer and expecting
the upper pull request to shrink by

174
00:12:36,110 --> 00:12:40,740
itself. Root cause: the squash has no
ancestry to the lower branch; without the

175
00:12:40,780 --> 00:12:44,700
preview feature GitHub only retargets,
and the transplant is yours.

176
00:12:45,040 --> 00:12:48,021
Building a release process
on stacked pull requests.

177
00:12:48,061 --> 00:12:50,577
Root cause: the feature
is a public preview.

178
00:12:52,417 --> 00:12:57,720
Now, out of the lab. An LLM platform team
splits a large change into two layers: a

179
00:12:57,760 --> 00:13:01,369
new priority-routing module,
and SLA timers that use it.

180
00:13:01,409 --> 00:13:06,778
The second engineer starts the upper layer
the same day, based on the lower branch.

181
00:13:06,818 --> 00:13:11,730
Reviews go faster. Then the team hits
both events of this video in one week.

182
00:13:12,070 --> 00:13:16,877
Monday, a review fix on the lower layer:
the upper engineer restacks with one git

183
00:13:16,917 --> 00:13:21,665
rebase and a --force-with-lease push, and
re-requests approval, because the diff

184
00:13:21,705 --> 00:13:22,144
changed.

185
00:13:22,484 --> 00:13:27,510
Thursday, the lower layer is squash-merged,
which is the repository's only allowed

186
00:13:27,550 --> 00:13:32,206
method, and its branch is deleted. The
upper pull request suddenly lists six

187
00:13:32,246 --> 00:13:37,149
commits. The engineer recognizes the
picture, runs git rebase --onto origin/main

188
00:13:37,189 --> 00:13:41,597
with the old lower branch as the
upstream, checks the range, and pushes.

189
00:13:41,937 --> 00:13:46,398
For their audit trail they write one sentence
into the review policy: the approval

190
00:13:46,438 --> 00:13:50,735
of record for a commit is the approval
on the pull request that carried it onto

191
00:13:50,775 --> 00:13:54,852
main. And they do not merge branches
that contain unreviewed pull requests.

192
00:13:56,692 --> 00:14:01,582
Your turn. Do Exercise 21.3, "A stack,
and a squash underneath it", in

193
00:14:01,622 --> 00:14:07,218
exercises/m19-m25-github.md. Before each
step, write down the base and the merge

194
00:14:07,258 --> 00:14:11,867
base of the upper pull request and
the number of commits it lists.

195
00:14:11,907 --> 00:14:17,291
After the squash, predict the number
before you ask Git, and write the --onto

196
00:14:17,331 --> 00:14:20,883
command with its three
arguments before you run it.

197
00:14:21,223 --> 00:14:26,659
The challenge is Exercise 21.6, "Five
commits for a two-commit fix", in the same

198
00:14:26,699 --> 00:14:27,001
file.

199
00:14:28,841 --> 00:14:32,222
Question 277 of the CTO question bank:

200
00:14:34,562 --> 00:14:39,286
Answer out loud. A strong answer describes
the upper pull request by its base, its

201
00:14:39,326 --> 00:14:44,051
merge base and its commit list after the
event. It separates what GitHub does with

202
00:14:44,091 --> 00:14:48,699
the preview feature from what it does
without it, and gives the Git command that

203
00:14:48,739 --> 00:14:52,999
finishes the job. On the last part it
takes a position and bases it on the

204
00:14:53,039 --> 00:14:55,730
documented status of the
feature, not on taste.

205
00:14:57,570 --> 00:15:02,301
Let's land this. You can now explain a
"merged" badge that nobody clicked, and keep

206
00:15:02,341 --> 00:15:04,486
a stack standing when a layer changes.

207
00:15:04,826 --> 00:15:06,486
You should now be able to say:

208
00:15:06,826 --> 00:15:11,397
A pull request is marked merged when its
head commits become reachable from its

209
00:15:11,437 --> 00:15:12,915
base, whoever merged what.

210
00:15:13,255 --> 00:15:17,350
Reviews and checks belong to pull
requests; reachability belongs to commits.

211
00:15:17,690 --> 00:15:22,330
In a stack each pull request's base is the
branch below, and linear means the lower

212
00:15:22,370 --> 00:15:24,304
branch is an ancestor of the upper.

